\details{
A fully connected feedforward network built and trained with \code{torch}, for
regression, binary and multiclass classification.

\strong{Architecture.} Give the hidden layers directly with \code{hidden_units} --
\code{c(256L, 128L, 64L)} is three layers of those widths -- or leave it NULL and
let \code{shape}, \code{shape_layers} and \code{shape_max_units} generate them. Setting both
is an error rather than a silent override. The generated profiles are
\code{funnel} (a linear taper from the widest layer down to a third of it),
\code{constant}, \code{triangle} (a linear rise from the input width), \code{long_funnel},
\code{diamond}, \code{hexagon} and \code{stairs}; the vocabulary is Talos's, by way of
AutoPyTorch. \code{shape_max_units} defaults to four times the encoded input
width, clamped to [64, 512] and never below that width.

The tabular deep-learning benchmarks tune and publish \emph{constant}-width MLPs,
so \code{shape = "constant"} is what to compare against even though \code{funnel} is
the better default before tuning on the small-n, wide-p data rtemis is usually
pointed at.

\strong{Tuning.} \code{hidden_units} is tunable like any other hyperparameter, with one
architecture per candidate:
\code{setup_MLP(hidden_units = tune_over(c(64L, 32L), c(128L, 64L, 32L)))}. A
single bare vector there is one architecture, not a set of candidates, and is
rejected as such.

\strong{Categorical features} are represented by learned embeddings, each sized
\code{min(600, round(1.6 * cardinality^0.56))} unless \code{embedding_dim} fixes them
all. \code{embeddings = FALSE} one-hot encodes instead. Numeric features are
always centered and scaled -- an unscaled network fails quietly rather than
loudly -- and the fitted encoder is re-applied at predict time.

\strong{Early stopping} needs a validation set: pass \code{dat_validation} to \link{train},
and the fit keeps the weights of the best validation epoch rather than the
last. Without one it runs the full \code{max_epochs} and \code{patience} has no effect.

\strong{\code{l1_penalty} is not \code{weight_decay}.} \code{weight_decay} is the L2 term the
torch optimizer applies, decoupled from the gradient under \code{adamw};
\code{l1_penalty} has no torch equivalent and is accumulated over the linear
weights and added to the loss.

\strong{Batch normalization and dropout interact badly} when combined; both are
off by default.

\strong{The scheduler takes no settings of its own} -- each configures itself from
the run's budget: \code{step} decays by 0.1 every \code{max_epochs / 3} epochs,
\code{cosine_annealing} anneals over \code{max_epochs}, \code{one_cycle} peaks at \code{lr} over
the run's real step count, and \code{reduce_on_plateau} decays by 0.1 after half
the early-stopping patience.

\code{get_varimp()} returns NULL: a torch MLP has no native importance measure.
}
